    summary: "ReDoS exploits regex patterns with catastrophic backtracking, where certain crafted inputs cause exponential evaluation time and hang the process on a single request.",
    explanation: "Patterns with nested quantifiers or ambiguous overlapping repetition, like (a+)+ or (a|a)*, can force the regex engine to try an exponential number of ways to match (or fail to match) a string, so a carefully crafted input of modest length can take minutes or hours to evaluate. Since Node.js and most single-threaded regex engines run the match synchronously, a single malicious request can block the entire event loop, taking down the server for all users, not just the attacker's connection. The fix is to rewrite ambiguous patterns to remove nested quantifiers, use possessive quantifiers or atomic groups where supported, impose input length limits before regex evaluation, and use timeout-based or non-backtracking regex engines (like RE2) for untrusted input. Tools like safe-regex or eslint-plugin-security can catch risky patterns in code review.",
    code: "// Vulnerable: nested quantifier causes catastrophic backtracking\nconst emailRegex = /^([a-zA-Z0-9]+)+@[a-zA-Z0-9]+\\.[a-zA-Z]+$/;\n\n// Attack input hangs the event loop\nconst evilInput = 'a'.repeat(40) + '!'; // no @, forces backtracking\nemailRegex.test(evilInput); // can take extremely long\n\n// Fixed: no nested quantifier, linear-time matching\nconst safeEmailRegex = /^[a-zA-Z0-9]+@[a-zA-Z0-9]+\\.[a-zA-Z]+$/;\n\n// Additional defense: cap input length before testing\nfunction safeTest(regex, input, maxLen = 254) {\n  if (input.length > maxLen) return false;\n  return regex.test(input);\n}",
