    id: "websecurity-zero-trust-basics",
    category: "websecurity",
    difficulty: "Advanced",
    topic: "Architecture",
    title: "What does 'never trust, always verify' mean in a Zero Trust architecture?",
    summary: "Zero Trust assumes no implicit trust based on network location; every request is authenticated, authorized, and encrypted regardless of whether it originates inside or outside the corporate network.",
    explanation: "Traditional perimeter security trusts anything inside the corporate network/VPN by default, which fails badly once an attacker breaches the perimeter (phishing, compromised laptop) and can then move laterally with minimal friction. Zero Trust instead requires continuous verification of identity and device posture for every request, enforces least-privilege access scoped narrowly to what's needed (not broad network-level access), and assumes breach by segmenting resources so a single compromised credential or service can't reach everything. Practical building blocks include strong identity providers with MFA, short-lived credentials instead of standing access, mutual TLS between services, and policy engines that evaluate context (device health, location, behavior) on every request rather than once at login. It's an architectural philosophy more than a single product, though tools like identity-aware proxies and service meshes implement pieces of it.",
    code: "// Traditional: trust anything inside the VPN/network\n// (no additional check once past the network perimeter)\n\n// Zero Trust: verify identity + device + context on every request\nasync function authorizeRequest(req) {\n  const identity = await verifyIdentityToken(req.headers.authorization);\n  const deviceOk = await checkDevicePosture(req.headers['x-device-id']);\n  const contextOk = evaluatePolicy({\n    identity,\n    resource: req.path,\n    ipRisk: await getIpRiskScore(req.ip),\n  });\n  if (!identity || !deviceOk || !contextOk) {\n    throw new Error('Access denied by zero trust policy engine');\n  }\n  return identity;\n}",
    interviewQuestion: "How does Zero Trust architecture change the blast radius of a single compromised employee laptop compared to a traditional perimeter-based network?",
