    title: "What are the main categories of DDoS attacks and how are they mitigated?",
    summary: "DDoS attacks overwhelm a service with traffic from many sources; defenses span network-layer volumetric filtering, protocol-level protections, and application-layer rate limiting and caching.",
    explanation: "Volumetric attacks (e.g., UDP floods, DNS amplification) try to saturate network bandwidth and are typically absorbed by upstream scrubbing centers or CDN/anycast networks with far more capacity than the origin server. Protocol attacks (e.g., SYN floods) exploit weaknesses in connection-handling to exhaust server resources like connection tables, mitigated with SYN cookies and connection rate limiting at the network edge. Application-layer attacks (e.g., HTTP floods hitting an expensive search endpoint) look like legitimate traffic and are harder to distinguish, requiring rate limiting, CAPTCHA challenges, bot detection heuristics, and aggressive caching/CDN offloading so the origin server never sees the bulk of requests. Layered defense — CDN/WAF in front, rate limiting at the app, autoscaling for legitimate spikes, and a incident response runbook — is standard practice rather than relying on any single control.",
    code: "// Application-layer mitigation: rate limit + CDN/WAF in front\n// CDN (e.g., Cloudflare) absorbs volumetric/protocol attacks\n// before traffic ever reaches origin servers.\n\n// App-level defense for expensive endpoints\nconst rateLimit = require('express-rate-limit');\nconst searchLimiter = rateLimit({\n  windowMs: 60 * 1000,\n  max: 20,\n  message: 'Too many requests, please slow down.',\n});\napp.get('/api/search', searchLimiter, handleSearch);\n\n// Cache expensive results to reduce load per request\napp.get('/api/reports/:id', cacheMiddleware(300), handleReport);",
